{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "אעברה נא", :translit "e'evrah na", :gloss "let me cross over, please"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "אעברה נא", :b "נ' אמה"}], :frame nil} {:quotes [{:he "והלבנן", :translit "vehalvanon", :gloss "and the Lebanon"} {:he "והלבנון", :translit "vehalvanon", :gloss "and the Lebanon (full spelling)"} {:he "ואתחנן", :translit "va'etchanan", :gloss "and I implored"} {:he "לעולה", :translit "le'olah", :gloss "for burnt offering"} {:he "למנחה", :translit "leminchah", :gloss "for meal offering"} {:he "לחטאת", :translit "lechatat", :gloss "for sin offering"} {:he "לאשם", :translit "le'asham", :gloss "for guilt offering"} {:he "למלואים", :translit "lemilu'im", :gloss "for ordination offerings"} {:he "לזבח השלמים", :translit "lezevach hashelamim", :gloss "for sacrifice of peace offerings"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "והלבנן written deficient (missing vav) alludes to six categories of offerings in 'this Torah' passage"} {:kind :masorah, :count 40, :occurrences []}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'this Torah' in Deuteronomy 4:44 introduces the section listing offerings; the six categories correspond to the sacrificial types enumerated in the wilderness (Numbers 28–29). Moses stood before God on Mount Sinai forty days and forty nights (Exodus 24:18, Deuteronomy 9:18). The practice of arranging praise of God before petitioning in prayer is derived from Moses, who opened with praise before making his request, as taught in Berakhot 32a.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 4:44" "Numbers 28–29" "Exodus 24:18" "Deuteronomy 9:18" "Berakhot 32a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "את גדלך", :translit "et gadlekha", :gloss "Your greatness"} {:he "אתה גבור", :translit "attah gibbor", :gloss "You are mighty"} {:he "אשר מי אל בשמים", :translit "asher mi el bashamayim", :gloss "for who is El in heaven"} {:he "אתה קדוש", :translit "attah kadosh", :gloss "You are holy"} {:he "אעברה נא", :translit "e'evrah na", :gloss "let me cross over, please"}], :mechanisms [], :frame {:text "One should always arrange praise of God first and then pray; this is learned from Moses, who began with praise — 'Your greatness' and 'Your strong hand' (Deuteronomy 3:24) — before making his personal request 'Let me cross over' (Deuteronomy 3:25). The early sages established that the first three blessings of the Amida are praises of God, and one must not ask personal needs in them, as stated in Mishna Berakhot 5:1 and expounded in Berakhot 34a. The phrase 'You are holy' parallels the language of the Kedusha rooted in Isaiah 6:3.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 3:24" "Deuteronomy 3:25" "Mishna Berakhot 5:1" "Berakhot 34a" "Isaiah 6:3"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "אעברה נא", :a-value 329, :b "נ' אמה", :b-value 96, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :other, :note "והלבנן written deficient (missing vav) alludes to six categories of offerings in 'this Torah' passage", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 40, :occurrences [], :by :selah, :located []}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:34:26.856715815Z", :model "glm-5.2"}